---
title: Manifolds and retractions
summary: skoro-riemann. The Manifold trait as a pair of differentiable operations (retraction and inverse retraction), the flat manifolds, and dyn-compatible erasure.
order: 4
katex_macros:
  \man: \mathcal{M}
---

A manifold, to skoro, is exactly two differentiable operations plus a dimension:

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-riemann/src/base.rs:5..30}
```

- The **retraction** $\retr : \man \times \T_x\man \to \man$ moves a point along a tangent vector.
  Every optimizer step and every perturbation goes through it.
- The **inverse retraction** $\invretr : \man \times \man \to \T_x\man$ gives the tangent vector
  between two points. A residual $x \ominus \mu = \invretr(\mu, x)$ is built from it.

Both are `DiffOperation`s, so they are differentiated like anything else in the graph. This is why
`skoro-riemann` depends on `skoro-operation`, and not the other way round.

## Manifolds as type tags

A type implementing `Manifold` is also a *tag*: `Q<B, SO3>` and `Q<B, Real>` are different types,
so operations are type-gated even though the tensors underneath are untyped. `DynManifold` is the
dyn-compatible twin, used where the graph must handle a quantity whose type is known only at run
time.

## The flat manifolds

`skoro-riemann` itself provides the flat cases, where both operations are addition and subtraction:
`Real`, `Euclidean<D>` / `EuclideanD`, and `EuclideanMatrix`. The curved groups live in their own
crates (`skoro-so3`, `skoro-se3`), and the lower-triangular factors `Tril` live in `skoro-linalg`.

